WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:13.278
Thank you.

00:00:13.278 --> 00:00:14.820
It's great to be
back with everybody.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:17.580
It's been quite a while since
I've seen so many faces.

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:18.810
And this is awesome.

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:20.930
I love being here and I
love being here with you.

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:21.600
Like Tatu said,

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:23.683
the name of my talk
is R, Python, and Tableau

00:00:23.683 --> 00:00:24.890
A Love Triangle

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:27.960
Some of you may recognize this
goes back to a few years ago.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:30.470
We ran it, we did a talk and
we did an advertising campaign

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:32.689
around R and Python,
a love story.

00:00:32.689 --> 00:00:34.460
And as I was getting
ready for this talk,

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:36.090
this title just seemed
to write itself.

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:38.282
And I was like, this is
just too good to pass up on.

00:00:38.282 --> 00:00:40.490
And then I started preparing
the talk and I was like,

00:00:40.490 --> 00:00:42.380
love triangles
are kind of awful.

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:43.680
They don't end well.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:45.360
Like nothing works
out. Like this--

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:47.090
Trying to make an
analogy between a love

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:49.010
triangle and these
three different tools

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:51.230
that I hope to illustrate
work really well together

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:52.710
wasn't working out for me.

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:53.930
And so we're going
to pivot a little bit

00:00:53.930 --> 00:00:55.320
and we'll actually be
talking a little bit,

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.440
we'll be drawing some
comparisons to music today,

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:00.090
and I hope that's OK.

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:02.590
So to start, we're going listen
to a couple of audio samples

00:01:02.590 --> 00:01:04.599
from perhaps one of
the most famous pieces

00:01:04.599 --> 00:01:05.420
of classical music.

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:06.640
You'll likely recognize
it even if you

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:09.220
don't know the name or the
composer or anything like that.

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:10.240
We'll see if this works.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:11.865
So we'll listen to
this first one here.

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:39.400
So some of you may recognize
that that's the beginning

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:40.680
of Beethoven's Fifth symphony.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:42.670
Some of the most famous
pieces, famous measures

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:43.978
of classical music ever heard.

00:01:43.978 --> 00:01:45.520
Now we're going to
listen to one more

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:47.528
version of those same
few measures of music

00:01:47.528 --> 00:01:48.570
and then compare the two.

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:19.820
OK. Same music, same notes.

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:21.020
Slightly different.

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:22.670
In the first case, we just
have the piano and nothing

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:23.420
against the piano.

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:24.040
I'm a pianist.

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:24.540
I have

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:26.498
I've been a pianist since
I was five years old.

00:02:26.498 --> 00:02:28.610
I love the piano,
but it can't quite

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:31.190
compare to the richness
of a full symphony.

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:32.930
As we add additional
musical instruments,

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:34.640
as we layer in
additional components,

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:36.420
the depth of the music grows.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:38.810
And we can hear that
in the two examples

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:40.340
we just looked at.
Today, we're going

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:42.300
to look at how we can do
the same with our analyses.

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:44.092
If we start with a
basic Tableau dashboard,

00:02:44.092 --> 00:02:46.160
how can we add additional
tools and bring

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:48.110
additional technologies
to that dashboard

00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:49.640
to add additional
depth of insight

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:52.870
to what we're delivering
to our business users?

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:54.670
Here's kind of an
example dashboard.

00:02:54.670 --> 00:02:57.850
This is a pretty straightforward
data exploration dashboard

00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:00.410
that allows a user to
explore a given data set.

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:02.350
I can choose an x axis,
I can choose a y axis,

00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:05.000
I can explore the relationship
between two features.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:07.030
In this case, this data
set is a collection

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:09.330
of attributes from
different pieces of music,

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:11.080
different genres of
music, different songs

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:12.140
from those genres.

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:13.400
How popular were they?

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:14.480
How danceable are they?

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:15.710
How likable are they?

00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:17.570
What the tempo is,
things of that nature.

00:03:17.570 --> 00:03:20.710
So I can create comparisons
between classical music and hip hop

00:03:20.710 --> 00:03:24.500
or instrumental music or
electronic music if I want to.

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:26.740
And a user can come in
here and explore and start

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:30.580
to determine their own insights
and arrive at conclusions based

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:32.680
on what they're looking
at in this data.

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:35.350
Now, if we wanted to take
this to another level

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:37.090
and add additional
details to it,

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:39.340
we might want to look to
add some machine learning

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:40.160
capabilities here.

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:42.510
What if we tried to predict
the popularity of a song

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:43.760
based on different attributes?

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:46.180
And how could we evaluate
the effectiveness

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:48.112
of different models
we might be using?

00:03:48.112 --> 00:03:49.570
To kind of illustrate
this example,

00:03:49.570 --> 00:03:50.720
I'm actually going
to turn to my son.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:53.500
I have a two-year-old son he
just turned two a couple of days

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:54.080
ago.

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:55.160
His name is Forrest.

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:55.960
Here he is.

00:03:55.960 --> 00:03:57.710
This picture was taken
just before I left.

00:03:57.710 --> 00:04:00.860
And as you can tell from this
photo, he's a bit random.

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:04.900
So we are going to be training
a random forest model today.

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:07.790
In R, what this might look
like is something like this.

00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:09.790
We'll bring in a package
like the ranger package

00:04:09.790 --> 00:04:12.220
and we can write a simple
function that takes some input

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:16.120
data (x and y), trains a model,
spits out the predicted values

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:18.251
based on the training
data that we supplied.

00:04:18.251 --> 00:04:19.959
Now, for all the
machine learning experts

00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:22.550
in the audience, this is not
a course on machine learning.

00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:24.970
So please recognize that
this is perhaps a bit overly

00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:27.940
simplistic, but it should help
us highlight and understand

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:31.240
how these different components
can all fit together.

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:34.240
If I wrote a function
like this in R and that

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:36.620
I wanted that function to be
accessible to other tools.

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:38.995
Right we're going to ignore
Tableau for a moment and just

00:04:38.995 --> 00:04:42.760
say, look, I want some generic
other service to have access

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:44.560
to this thing that I wrote.

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:46.570
The best and easiest
way to do that in R

00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:48.792
is using the plumber package,
and here's an example

00:04:48.792 --> 00:04:50.000
of what that might look like.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:51.590
If I bring in the
plumber package,

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:54.130
first of all, I have to bring
the package into my workspace,

00:04:54.130 --> 00:04:57.940
and then all I need to do is
add a few special comments

00:04:57.940 --> 00:04:59.890
to my script that
indicate, hey, this

00:04:59.890 --> 00:05:03.280
should be an API that's
listening for requests

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:04.920
from other services.

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:06.670
It's very straightforward
and easy to use,

00:05:06.670 --> 00:05:09.412
especially if I'm already
used to using R.

00:05:09.412 --> 00:05:11.870
Now let's take that and make
it a little bit more specific.

00:05:11.870 --> 00:05:14.560
Let's say that instead of just
any generic service out there,

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:17.270
I actually want this to be
used from within Tableau.

00:05:17.270 --> 00:05:20.983
I want Tableau to be able to
submit data to this function.

00:05:20.983 --> 00:05:22.900
And I want this function
to execute and return

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:24.400
the data back to Tableau.

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:28.690
Well, I could write my own
very unique API to do that,

00:05:28.690 --> 00:05:32.000
or I could use the
plumbertableau package, which

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:34.000
handles a lot of the
specifics behind the scenes

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:37.540
to make this API work,
specifically with Tableau

00:05:37.540 --> 00:05:39.220
workbooks and dashboards.

00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:41.470
As you notice, again, I've
highlighted the differences

00:05:41.470 --> 00:05:43.240
here between a
traditional plumber

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:47.000
API and this new plumbertableau
API, and they're very minimal.

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:48.740
I bring in the
plumbertableau package.

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:51.820
I had a few additional comments
that indicate what type of data

00:05:51.820 --> 00:05:53.950
I'm expecting from
tableau, what type of data

00:05:53.950 --> 00:05:55.610
I'm going to be
returning to Tableau.

00:05:55.610 --> 00:05:57.220
And then the most
significant piece

00:05:57.220 --> 00:05:58.660
is that piece
there at the bottom

00:05:58.660 --> 00:06:04.438
where I indicate that I'm
creating a Tableau extension.

00:06:04.438 --> 00:06:06.230
If we do the same thing
on the Python side,

00:06:06.230 --> 00:06:07.680
we could bring in scikit-learn.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:09.950
We could define a function
that creates and trains

00:06:09.950 --> 00:06:11.660
a random forest
and again spits out

00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:13.850
the predicted outcomes
based on the input data

00:06:13.850 --> 00:06:15.338
that we received.

00:06:15.338 --> 00:06:17.630
If I want to make this a
generically available service,

00:06:17.630 --> 00:06:19.470
I could use a tool
like FastAPI.

00:06:19.470 --> 00:06:21.400
I can bring in the
FastAPI module.

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:23.630
I can define an app and
then I can define routes

00:06:23.630 --> 00:06:25.370
on that app that are
functions that are

00:06:25.370 --> 00:06:27.560
listening for incoming input.

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:30.080
Just like we saw on the R
side with plumbertableau,

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:32.390
there's a new package
called FastAPITableau

00:06:32.390 --> 00:06:33.467
that does the same thing.

00:06:33.467 --> 00:06:35.300
In fact, it's maybe
even a little bit easier

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:37.175
than what we saw on the
plumbertableau side,

00:06:37.175 --> 00:06:38.640
because all I need
to do is change

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:40.800
import FastAPI to
import FastAPITableau

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:43.790
and I need to change
my app instantiation instead

00:06:43.790 --> 00:06:47.070
of saying it's a Fast API app,
I now just say it's a Fast API

00:06:47.070 --> 00:06:47.760
Tableau app.

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:49.135
Everything else
remains the same.

00:06:51.710 --> 00:06:54.590
Now, once I've developed
these extensions,

00:06:54.590 --> 00:06:57.848
I might have used RStudio
Workbench to develop these.

00:06:57.848 --> 00:06:59.390
I might have developed
these locally,

00:06:59.390 --> 00:07:01.700
but once I've developed
them, I need somewhere

00:07:01.700 --> 00:07:03.920
where they can be hosted
so that I can access them

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:05.150
from within Tableau.

00:07:05.150 --> 00:07:08.247
The easiest place to do
that is RStudio Connect.

00:07:08.247 --> 00:07:09.830
And here's an example
of what it looks

00:07:09.830 --> 00:07:12.950
like when I've published these
two extensions onto RStudio

00:07:12.950 --> 00:07:13.520
Connect.

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:15.020
The first thing I'm
going to look at

00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:16.760
is I'm going to look
at the Python extension

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:18.260
that we built. And
once I come in here,

00:07:18.260 --> 00:07:20.802
you'll see that I have a nice
user interface that illustrates

00:07:20.802 --> 00:07:23.690
to Tableau users exactly what
they need to do if they want

00:07:23.690 --> 00:07:25.123
to use this extension.

00:07:25.123 --> 00:07:27.290
There's also a nice feature
for the developer, which

00:07:27.290 --> 00:07:29.930
will allow me to come in
here and test the API out,

00:07:29.930 --> 00:07:32.190
make sure the extension is
doing what I want it to do.

00:07:32.190 --> 00:07:33.570
Does it take data appropriately?

00:07:33.570 --> 00:07:35.702
Does it return the
appropriate response?

00:07:35.702 --> 00:07:37.910
Does it behave the way that
I think it should behave?

00:07:37.910 --> 00:07:41.720
This is really nice because
as an R or a Python developer,

00:07:41.720 --> 00:07:43.970
this means I never
have to open Tableau.

00:07:43.970 --> 00:07:45.620
I can start from
the beginning and I

00:07:45.620 --> 00:07:49.040
can go all the way through to a
working, functioning extension

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:49.950
of Tableau.

00:07:49.950 --> 00:07:51.350
But I never have
to actually open

00:07:51.350 --> 00:07:53.360
Tableau to verify its behavior.

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:55.190
If we look at the
R extension, here's

00:07:55.190 --> 00:07:57.030
the R extension we published.

00:07:57.030 --> 00:07:58.080
We see a similar thing.

00:07:58.080 --> 00:07:59.600
We have a nice
interface that lets

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:01.487
us know how to use this
from within tableau,

00:08:01.487 --> 00:08:03.320
and we'll take a look
at that implementation

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:04.170
in just a moment.

00:08:04.170 --> 00:08:06.860
And I also have the ability,
like we saw on the Python side,

00:08:06.860 --> 00:08:10.130
to test and verify the
behavior of this particular

00:08:10.130 --> 00:08:11.540
of this particular extension.

00:08:11.540 --> 00:08:13.860
I can copy output data
from somewhere else.

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:15.830
I can drop it in
here and I can verify

00:08:15.830 --> 00:08:18.080
that this is, in fact
returning the results that I

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:19.708
would expect it to
be returning, given

00:08:19.708 --> 00:08:20.875
the function that I defined.

00:08:24.170 --> 00:08:26.510
Now, if we look at how to
do this within tableau,

00:08:26.510 --> 00:08:28.910
we've tried to make
this as easy as possible

00:08:28.910 --> 00:08:31.760
for somebody who maybe isn't
an R or Python developer,

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:34.909
but is a Tableau user to
be able to come in and use

00:08:34.909 --> 00:08:36.350
an extension that
has been shared

00:08:36.350 --> 00:08:38.424
on a platform like
RStudio Connect.

00:08:38.424 --> 00:08:39.799
In that case, the
user might come

00:08:39.799 --> 00:08:41.630
in to this particular page.

00:08:41.630 --> 00:08:43.789
They'll highlight and
copy the usage section

00:08:43.789 --> 00:08:45.170
of the documentation.

00:08:45.170 --> 00:08:49.190
They'll move over into tableau,
create a new calculated field,

00:08:49.190 --> 00:08:51.330
give that calculated
field some sort of a name.

00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:53.663
In this case, we'll call this
random forest or something

00:08:53.663 --> 00:08:56.880
to that effect, and then paste
in the data that they copied

00:08:56.880 --> 00:08:58.910
or the script that
they copied from

00:08:58.910 --> 00:09:01.015
the previous documentation.

00:09:01.015 --> 00:09:03.390
Once this is copied in, the
only thing that we need to do

00:09:03.390 --> 00:09:06.090
is we need to supply the
actual names of fields

00:09:06.090 --> 00:09:09.120
that we want Tableau to
supply to this extension.

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:10.840
In this case, I
was very creative.

00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:13.320
And so we have
X axis and Y axis,

00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:15.450
but that allows us
to simply identify

00:09:15.450 --> 00:09:17.550
what the x and y variables
are that we're passing

00:09:17.550 --> 00:09:19.890
in to our underlying model.

00:09:19.890 --> 00:09:22.360
Once this is done, I can
use the calculated field.

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:24.900
I can drag it into my workbook
and use it just as I would

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:28.460
any other variable or
feature within my data set.

00:09:28.460 --> 00:09:30.500
So if we bring all of
these different components

00:09:30.500 --> 00:09:33.140
together now, right, we have
this original Tableau dashboard

00:09:33.140 --> 00:09:35.330
that allows users to
explore data, defined

00:09:35.330 --> 00:09:38.060
x and y-axes, filtered to
specific genres of music,

00:09:38.060 --> 00:09:40.610
identify relationships between
these different features

00:09:40.610 --> 00:09:42.443
that they might be
interested in looking in.

00:09:42.443 --> 00:09:44.155
And then we have these
two new extensions

00:09:44.155 --> 00:09:45.030
that we've developed.

00:09:45.030 --> 00:09:46.670
Both are training
random forest models,

00:09:46.670 --> 00:09:48.890
but one is done in Python,
and one is done in R,

00:09:48.890 --> 00:09:52.660
and they're hosted
on RStudio Connect.

00:09:52.660 --> 00:09:54.580
If we take a look at-- if we take a look

00:09:54.580 --> 00:09:55.960
at all these pieces
put together,

00:09:55.960 --> 00:09:57.780
we might have something
that looks like this.

00:09:57.780 --> 00:09:59.440
Our original dashboard
is up at the top

00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:01.810
where users can still
define x and y axes,

00:10:01.810 --> 00:10:04.060
investigate relationships
between different components

00:10:04.060 --> 00:10:05.000
within the data,

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:07.540
but then down below, we've
added these two new plots

00:10:07.540 --> 00:10:10.720
that indicate the residual
plots from our underlying random

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:11.970
forest models that we trained.

00:10:11.970 --> 00:10:14.350
On the left, we see our
random forest model in R.

00:10:14.350 --> 00:10:17.210
On the right, we see our
random forest model in Python.

00:10:17.210 --> 00:10:20.680
And you'll see as I interact
with this particular dashboard,

00:10:20.680 --> 00:10:23.950
these everything is
dynamic, everything updates

00:10:23.950 --> 00:10:26.780
as I make changes and
adjustments to the dashboard.

00:10:26.780 --> 00:10:29.710
So if I choose to bring
electronic music into the mix,

00:10:29.710 --> 00:10:32.200
I'll see that first reflected
in the plot up above

00:10:32.200 --> 00:10:34.870
and then I'll see it also
reflected in the plots

00:10:34.870 --> 00:10:36.290
below as new data--

00:10:36.290 --> 00:10:38.150
Once again, this is a
real time interaction.

00:10:38.150 --> 00:10:41.140
So new data is being
submitted from Tableau

00:10:41.140 --> 00:10:42.680
to these extensions,

00:10:42.680 --> 00:10:44.242
some sort of
execution is happening,

00:10:44.242 --> 00:10:46.450
and then the results are
being transmitted right back

00:10:46.450 --> 00:10:49.210
into Tableau for
further analysis.

00:10:49.210 --> 00:10:52.097
Now, this use case is
a little bit specific,

00:10:52.097 --> 00:10:54.430
and the use case that we've
kind of focused on primarily

00:10:54.430 --> 00:10:58.720
here is I'm in tableau, but I
need to do something to my data

00:10:58.720 --> 00:10:59.720
that Tableau can't do.

00:10:59.720 --> 00:11:02.230
And in many cases that might be
like something machine learning

00:11:02.230 --> 00:11:03.730
or machine learning
adjacent, right?

00:11:03.730 --> 00:11:06.130
I need to do some sort
of statistical procedure

00:11:06.130 --> 00:11:09.590
or analysis that Tableau just
isn't fully equipped to do.

00:11:09.590 --> 00:11:12.405
And I've had good conversations
with people at Tableau that

00:11:12.405 --> 00:11:13.780
have indicated to
me, "Look, we're

00:11:13.780 --> 00:11:16.810
"not we're not trying to make
Tableau a fully featured

00:11:16.810 --> 00:11:18.590
statistical analysis engine."

00:11:18.590 --> 00:11:20.200
And so these
extensions are the way

00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:22.420
in which we can broaden the
horizons of what Tableau

00:11:22.420 --> 00:11:25.300
is capable of by allowing it
to plug into other tools that

00:11:25.300 --> 00:11:27.047
might be more effective
at certain tasks.

00:11:27.047 --> 00:11:29.380
And we certainly might all
agree that R and Python are

00:11:29.380 --> 00:11:32.260
two tools very well suited to
common statistical and machine

00:11:32.260 --> 00:11:33.750
learning tasks.

00:11:33.750 --> 00:11:35.500
There's another class
of problems, though,

00:11:35.500 --> 00:11:39.310
and that class of problems is
I have some data in Tableau

00:11:39.310 --> 00:11:42.040
and I have in my mind some sort
of visualization that I want

00:11:42.040 --> 00:11:45.185
to do, but I can't do it
or it's really difficult.

00:11:45.185 --> 00:11:46.810
And if you've ever
worked with Tableau,

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:49.060
I find myself in this position
pretty frequently where

00:11:49.060 --> 00:11:50.890
I can imagine some
sort of visualization,

00:11:50.890 --> 00:11:54.220
and then I find myself wishing
there was some sort of  ggplot

00:11:54.220 --> 00:11:56.910
Tableau integration thing.

00:11:56.910 --> 00:11:58.210
Well, we don't quite have that.

00:11:58.210 --> 00:12:01.470
What we do have is a package
called shinytableau.

00:12:01.470 --> 00:12:02.200
Shiny Tableau

00:12:02.200 --> 00:12:03.820
And I'm not going to go through
all of the details here.

00:12:03.820 --> 00:12:06.000
I'm going to provide links to
the documentation and things

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:07.750
that you can kind of
explore on your own.

00:12:07.750 --> 00:12:10.830
But shinytableau allows
you to define a custom Shiny

00:12:10.830 --> 00:12:14.370
application that creates a
unique extension for a Tableau

00:12:14.370 --> 00:12:15.103
workbook.

00:12:15.103 --> 00:12:16.770
What this looks like
is and on the left,

00:12:16.770 --> 00:12:18.930
I have this there's a
specific manifest file

00:12:18.930 --> 00:12:21.150
that you write when you
create these extensions.

00:12:21.150 --> 00:12:23.320
There's some nuance to how
this gets put together.

00:12:23.320 --> 00:12:24.270
And if you're
familiar with shiny,

00:12:24.270 --> 00:12:26.400
it will feel comfortable
and familiar to you.

00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:29.133
But if you're not quite
familiar with shiny, it's

00:12:29.133 --> 00:12:31.050
a good idea to start
with Shiny first and then

00:12:31.050 --> 00:12:34.098
look at shinytableau
as, as a second option.

00:12:34.098 --> 00:12:35.640
But there's this
manifest file that I

00:12:35.640 --> 00:12:37.680
create that defines the
extension and some attributes

00:12:37.680 --> 00:12:38.260
of it.

00:12:38.260 --> 00:12:39.802
And when I run that
Shiny application

00:12:39.802 --> 00:12:42.010
or when I publish that Shiny
application in somewhere

00:12:42.010 --> 00:12:43.800
like RStudio Connect,
what I end up with

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:45.263
is this page on the right.

00:12:45.263 --> 00:12:47.430
This doesn't look like any
sort of Shiny application

00:12:47.430 --> 00:12:49.050
you've likely seen before
because there's nothing

00:12:49.050 --> 00:12:50.260
really interactive here.

00:12:50.260 --> 00:12:53.880
Instead, what I have is a prompt
to download some sort of file.

00:12:53.880 --> 00:12:56.190
So what I do is I download
that file locally,

00:12:56.190 --> 00:12:58.170
and then if I'm using
Tableau desktop,

00:12:58.170 --> 00:13:00.578
I can import that file
directly into Tableau Desktop.

00:13:00.578 --> 00:13:02.370
If I'm using something
like Tableau Online,

00:13:02.370 --> 00:13:04.290
I would need to put
that file in a location

00:13:04.290 --> 00:13:06.630
where I can read it from my
Tableau Online or Tableau

00:13:06.630 --> 00:13:08.550
Server environment.

00:13:08.550 --> 00:13:11.250
So if we bring all this
together and take it once more

00:13:11.250 --> 00:13:13.600
from the top, here's what
that might look like.

00:13:13.600 --> 00:13:16.230
Here's the existing dashboard
that I have with my R and

00:13:16.230 --> 00:13:18.600
Python extensions operating
here on the bottom.

00:13:18.600 --> 00:13:21.750
And if I want to bring this
shinytableau extension in,

00:13:21.750 --> 00:13:24.360
what I need to do is drag an
extension onto my dashboard,

00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:26.682
navigate to that file
that I downloaded locally.

00:13:26.682 --> 00:13:28.140
So here we'll see
that I can select

00:13:28.140 --> 00:13:30.780
the file that was downloaded
from that Shiny application.

00:13:30.780 --> 00:13:32.460
Once that's
downloaded, I then need

00:13:32.460 --> 00:13:34.188
to configure the
extension itself.

00:13:34.188 --> 00:13:35.730
And these are all
components that you

00:13:35.730 --> 00:13:38.280
can adjust and set up when
you define the shinytableau

00:13:38.280 --> 00:13:39.325
extension.

00:13:39.325 --> 00:13:40.950
I'll come in here,
I'll select the data

00:13:40.950 --> 00:13:42.900
that I want this extension
to be reading from,

00:13:42.900 --> 00:13:45.510
and then I'll select the values
that I want Tableau to pass

00:13:45.510 --> 00:13:47.402
to this specific extension.

00:13:47.402 --> 00:13:49.860
In this case, what we're going
to do once we finish this is

00:13:49.860 --> 00:13:52.320
we'll have a nice density plot
over here on the right hand

00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:55.110
side that demonstrates
the density of everything

00:13:55.110 --> 00:13:56.080
along the y-axis.

00:13:56.080 --> 00:13:58.330
And I could do the same thing
to create a density plot

00:13:58.330 --> 00:14:00.823
along the x-axis as well,
which might be something

00:14:00.823 --> 00:14:03.240
that I want to give me a better
understanding of the shape

00:14:03.240 --> 00:14:05.730
of my data, especially when
I have so many data points

00:14:05.730 --> 00:14:07.350
that I'm considering,
like I have in

00:14:07.350 --> 00:14:09.178
this particular visualization.

00:14:09.178 --> 00:14:11.470
One other thing that's worth
mentioning that I haven't

00:14:11.470 --> 00:14:13.240
brought up to this
point is the fact

00:14:13.240 --> 00:14:17.020
that, again, we have both
R and Python extensions

00:14:17.020 --> 00:14:19.750
operating as part of the
same Tableau workbook.

00:14:19.750 --> 00:14:22.060
RStudio Connect
is the only endpoint

00:14:22.060 --> 00:14:25.570
that supports this idea of
both R and Python extensions

00:14:25.570 --> 00:14:27.390
simultaneously in
the same workbook.

00:14:27.390 --> 00:14:29.890
If you were to use something
more traditional like TabPy or

00:14:29.890 --> 00:14:33.670
Rserve, you would be restricted
only to Python execution

00:14:33.670 --> 00:14:35.648
or only to R execution.

00:14:35.648 --> 00:14:37.690
But given the work that
we've done with the folks

00:14:37.690 --> 00:14:40.390
at Tableau and the work
that we've done internally

00:14:40.390 --> 00:14:41.860
with RStudio
Connect, RStudio

00:14:41.860 --> 00:14:45.130
Connect can host R extensions

00:14:45.130 --> 00:14:46.772
and Python extensions
side by side.

00:14:46.772 --> 00:14:48.730
And they can be leveraged
from the same Tableau

00:14:48.730 --> 00:14:51.730
environment alongside things
like shinytableau extensions

00:14:51.730 --> 00:14:53.410
as well.

00:14:53.410 --> 00:14:55.450
So what does this
all mean? Going back

00:14:55.450 --> 00:14:56.633
to our music analogy,

00:14:56.633 --> 00:14:59.050
the idea here is we can use
the right combination of tools

00:14:59.050 --> 00:14:59.870
for the job,

00:14:59.870 --> 00:15:02.620
And we can deliver a symphony of
insight. More than just a piano

00:15:02.620 --> 00:15:03.120
solo,

00:15:03.120 --> 00:15:05.500
we can deliver something
that astounds and astonishes

00:15:05.500 --> 00:15:06.790
our end users.

00:15:06.790 --> 00:15:08.470
Now, is this necessary

00:15:08.470 --> 00:15:09.303
every time? It's not.

00:15:09.303 --> 00:15:11.803
I have a six-year-old daughter
who just had her first piano

00:15:11.803 --> 00:15:13.930
recital, and it was exactly
what it needed to be.

00:15:13.930 --> 00:15:17.170
She didn't need to be playing
the violin and the trumpet

00:15:17.170 --> 00:15:18.400
and everything all at once.

00:15:18.400 --> 00:15:20.260
The piano recital was enough.

00:15:20.260 --> 00:15:22.750
And it's up to us as the data
scientists, as the analysts,

00:15:22.750 --> 00:15:25.180
to decide what
level of insight is

00:15:25.180 --> 00:15:28.000
the appropriate level for the
analysis that we're doing.

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:29.500
It's important to
understand that we

00:15:29.500 --> 00:15:32.313
have a whole host of tools
like JJ kind of alluded

00:15:32.313 --> 00:15:33.480
to in his talk this morning.

00:15:33.480 --> 00:15:35.575
We continue to look
at this ecosystem

00:15:35.575 --> 00:15:37.450
and look for ways in
which we can participate

00:15:37.450 --> 00:15:39.970
in the broad scientific
computing ecosystem,

00:15:39.970 --> 00:15:42.460
and that includes tools like
Tableau and the integrations

00:15:42.460 --> 00:15:44.617
that we've done there.

00:15:44.617 --> 00:15:46.200
If you're interested
in learning more,

00:15:46.200 --> 00:15:47.470
there are some resources here.

00:15:47.470 --> 00:15:50.700
There's a GitHub repository that
has several different examples

00:15:50.700 --> 00:15:52.272
of how these different
extensions can

00:15:52.272 --> 00:15:54.730
work with different Tableau
workbooks and things like that.

00:15:54.730 --> 00:15:56.200
And you can learn
from those examples.

00:15:56.200 --> 00:15:58.075
The slides and all the
content from this talk

00:15:58.075 --> 00:16:00.100
will be made available
on GitHub shortly after

00:16:00.100 --> 00:16:01.880
I've kind of finished
everything up today.

00:16:01.880 --> 00:16:03.630
And finally, I just
want to say Thank you.

00:16:03.630 --> 00:16:05.260
It's been great
being back in person.

00:16:05.260 --> 00:16:08.420
And if there's any questions,
we can take those now.

